  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1205 PM MDT Wed Oct 28 2026\n\nMild fall conditions continue across the West Slope through\nThursday. A shift in the synoptic pattern begins to take shape\nThursday afternoon as low pressure drops into the PACNW. This\nwill send a southwesterly fetch of Pacific moisture into the\nSouthwest. High based cloud cover will begin to blanket the\nregion, while the low levels of the atmosphere stay dry into\nFriday. By Friday morning some weak orographic showers are\npossible for southeast UT and southwest CO. The scarcity of \nmoisture at this point will keep accumulations near trace\nvalues. Moisture continues to increase Friday and Saturday, \nwith light rain and snow possible through Saturday. Snow levels \nremain above 10000 feet with daytime temperatures keeping any \naccumulations in check. Below snow line, rainfall is expected to\nremain on the light side. However, a few isolated thunderstorms\nremain in the forecast. This could produce heavier showers on \nFriday and Saturday afternoon.\n\nBy Sunday, AR(atmospheric river) moisture begins to fill in\nacross the Southwest. The trajectory of this plume still favors\na more southerly track, keeping our CWA out of some of the\nheaviest rainfall potential. However, ensemble models continue\nto highlight an area of 1 to 2 inches of rainfall potential \nover southwest CO. On the heels of heavy rainfall last weekend, \nthis is expected to have some adverse effects on runoff near \nsaturated hillsides. River flooding continues to look unlikely \nat these values spread over 48 hours. However, localized impacts\nfrom mud, rocks, and debris flows on saturated hillsides are \nlikely. These impacts will seem more pronounced in areas still \ncleaning up last week's mess. Rainfall begins to peak early \nMonday morning as the frontal boundary swings through. This will\ndrop snow levels to around 8000 feet overnight and Monday \nmorning. Mountain passes will likely see some travel slowdowns \nfor motorists Monday morning and into the afternoon, as snow \nlevels drop more behind the front. Afternoon highs on Monday \nwill struggle to reach the 50s for many of our low valleys.\n\nColder, drier air continues to invade Monday night and early\nTuesday. Precipitation will taper off significantly Monday\nafternoon, but the shoulders of the western Divide will hang on\nto some light snow and rain into Tuesday morning. Light\norographic rain and snow continues on the terrain Wednesday, \nwith minimal additional accumulation.",
